  "statement_token": "\"Let $G$ be a graph with $n$ vertices and no isolated vertices. Let $alpha(G)$ be its stability number, $sigma_e(G)=omega_e(\\\\overline G)$ its edge-stable-set invariant, $omega(G)$ its clique number, and $omega_e(G)$ its edge-clique-covering number. **Ore's conjecture.** If\\n\\n$$\\nalpha(G)=sigma_e(G)\\\\qquad\\\\text{and}\\\\qquad omega(G)=omega_e(G),\\n$$\\n\\nthen\\n\\n$$\\nomega_e(G)sigma_e(G)\\\\leq n.\\n$$\\n\\nThe source presents this as an answer to an open question posed by Ore and as a variant for connected graphs of Turán's theorem; the supplied status is open.\"",
  "statement_token_sha256": "693c3c1908fb3b6c2a52f9bac2cc1276b97e5e3717ac1bb378f516daa777547f",
  "statement": "Let $G$ be a graph with $n$ vertices and no isolated vertices. Let $alpha(G)$ be its stability number, $sigma_e(G)=omega_e(\\overline G)$ its edge-stable-set invariant, $omega(G)$ its clique number, and $omega_e(G)$ its edge-clique-covering number. **Ore's conjecture.** If\n\n$$\nalpha(G)=sigma_e(G)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad omega(G)=omega_e(G),\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nomega_e(G)sigma_e(G)\\leq n.\n$$\n\nThe source presents this as an answer to an open question posed by Ore and as a variant for connected graphs of Turán's theorem; the supplied status is open.",
